  "summary": "No fresh wire this morning — so here is the thing the H1 numbers are telling every operator, and most are still not pricing for.",
  "bodyHtml": "<p><span class=\"meta-line\">🏨 EA HOSPITALITY PULSE — Morning Brief</span><br><span class=\"meta-line\">📅 Sunday, 9 August 2026 | 🇰🇪🇹🇿🇺🇬🇷🇼</span></p>\n<p>No fresh wire this morning — so here is the thing the H1 numbers are telling every operator, and most are still not pricing for.<br>━━━━━━━━━<br><span class=\"item-head\">1️⃣ THE GUESTS ARE ALREADY ACROSS THE BORDER</span><br>The half-year data is in, and it points one way. South Africa took 5.58m international arrivals in H1 2026, up 12.3% — but the split matters more than the headline: arrivals from the rest of Africa rose 14.3%, while overseas arrivals grew just 5.6% (Statistics South Africa, released 30 July). More than 70% of monthly arrivals came from the SADC region. The same pattern shows in our own backyard: Rwanda&#x27;s single largest source market in H1 was not Europe or America but the DRC — 185,420 visitors, 12.5% of arrivals — with Kenya (10%) and Tanzania (9%) next (Rwanda Development Board, H1 2026).<br>Regional demand is growing two to three times faster than long-haul, and it is the base that holds when a Western advisory lands or the dollar turns. Yet it is still treated as walk-in: undifferentiated rate, no product, no campaign.<br><span class=\"sowhat\">🎯 So what: build a deliberate regional book, don&#x27;t wait for it. City hotels — price a weekday cross-border corporate and long-stay rate for the trade, health and education travel that already fills midweek. Bush — sell the shoulder season on the African-resident and EAC tariff you already have (Uganda gorilla permits run US$500 for Africans and UGX 300,000 for EAC citizens vs US$800 foreign; low-season US$600). Beach — court the Gulf and India charter demand that fills the European off-months.</span><br><span class=\"tagline\">🏷 All | Regional/Rwanda/EA | Reported</span><br>━━━━━━━━━<br>📌 STILL TRUE (context, unchanged)<br>• Advisory board: US — Kenya L2, Uganda L4, Tanzania/Zanzibar L3, Rwanda L3. No weekend move.<br>• Cost: Kenya diesel KSh 222.86/L, super KSh 214.03/L held only through 14 Aug (EPRA July review). Lock transfer/generator contracts this week.</p>\n<p><span class=\"meta-line\">📡 THE WATCH</span><br>• Tue 12 Aug — US CDC §362 Ebola entry order expires; watch renewal + whether Uganda is decoupled<br>• Thu–Fri 14–15 Aug — EPRA fuel review reprices the cycle<br>• 19–21 Aug — Hotel Expo Kenya, KICC Nairobi — city-hotel midweek compression</p>\n<p><span class=\"meta-line\">💬 Are you quoting a distinct regional rate, or letting the border trade walk in at rack?</span></p>\n<p>🔗 This edition on the web: https://eahospitalitypulse.com/editions/pulse-2026-08-09-morning.html<br>— EA Hospitality Pulse | Daily intelligence for city, bush &amp; beach properties</p>"
